## Confirmed

Re-ran `crimes scan --format json` against the fixture; it reproduces the report above. Three duplication findings, all `medium`, plus two `low` clock findings that ride along with the telemetry duplicate:

| id | charge | what it caught |
|---|---|---|
| `crime_00001` | Near-Duplicate Block | `sanitizeUserInput` / `sanitizeText` / `cleanMessage` — same body hash `289f7da71f3a…`, 3 files |
| `crime_00002` | Exact Duplicate Block | `recordSanitiseEvent` — same body hash `4f23be2be37a…`, `telemetry-a.ts` + `telemetry-b.ts` |
| `crime_00003` | Duplicated Role/Status/Plan Check | literal `"admin"`, 4 files, **2 distinct expression shapes** |
| `crime_00004/5` | Temporal Recklessness | `Date.now()` at `telemetry-{a,b}.ts:16` |

I read the six source files to check the findings rather than take them on faith. Two things worth knowing before you refactor:

**The sanitiser triple is a true rename-only duplicate.** `sanitize-a.ts:10-36`, `sanitize-b.ts:9-35`, `sanitize-c.ts:9-35` differ only in the parameter and local variable names (`raw`/`working`, `input`/`cleaned`, `value`/`normalised`). Every threshold is identical — the 1000-char cap, the `[!?]{3,}` count > 5, the 0.9 upper-ratio at length > 30. There is **nothing** that needs parameterising, so `fix_shape`'s "pass the differing values as args" doesn't apply here: one nullary-options function reaches exact parity.

**The admin guards have already drifted — do not collapse them.** This is the finding that matters, and it's the one the JSON can only hint at ("2 distinct expression shapes"):

```ts
assertAdminA(role)  // role === "admin"
assertAdminB(role)  // role === "admin" || role === "owner"   ← wider
assertAdminC(role)  // role !== "admin"                        ← inverted
```

`assertAdminC` returns the **opposite** of its siblings despite the identical name shape. Any agent that "consolidates the admin checks" by picking one implementation silently flips or widens authorisation at a third of the call sites. Treat `crime_00003` as two separate jobs: dedupe `policy.ts`'s three identical bodies (safe), and *rebase* the sanitize-* guards onto a shared primitive while preserving each one's distinct semantics (not a dedupe).

## Proposed refactor

Two new modules, three deletions, one rewrite.

**`src/sanitize.ts`** (new) — the single implementation, body lifted verbatim from `sanitize-a.ts`.

**`src/telemetry.ts`** (new) — one `recordSanitiseEvent`, with the clock injected so `crime_00004/5` die with the duplicate rather than getting copied into the survivor.

**`src/policy.ts`** (rewrite) — `isAdmin` becomes the one place `"admin"` is compared, and the three `can*` functions plus the three sanitize-* guards all delegate to it.

### Public API of the consolidated functions

```ts
// src/sanitize.ts
export interface SanitizeOptions {
  maxLength?: number;        // default 1000
  maxPunctuationRuns?: number; // default 5   — runs of /[!?]{3,}/
  shoutRatio?: number;       // default 0.9   — uppercase share that triggers lowercasing
  shoutMinLength?: number;   // default 30    — below this, shouting is left alone
}

export function sanitize(input: string, options?: SanitizeOptions): string;
```

Call it with no options and you get byte-for-byte the current behaviour of all three functions — that's what makes the migration mechanical and reviewable. The options object exists because those four magic numbers are the things that will actually diverge next; better they diverge as arguments at a call site than as a fourth copy of the file. If you'd rather not speculate, ship `sanitize(input: string): string` and add the options when a caller needs them — the internals don't change either way.

```ts
// src/telemetry.ts
export interface SanitiseEvent {
  type: "sanitise.complete";
  payload: {
    originalLength: number;
    sanitisedLength: number;
    reductionRatio: number;
    timestamp: number;
  };
  source: "sanitiser";
}

/** Pure: builds the event, reads no clock. */
export function buildSanitiseEvent(
  originalLength: number,
  sanitisedLength: number,
  now: number,
): SanitiseEvent;

/** Side-effecting shell; `now` defaults to Date.now() at the boundary. */
export function recordSanitiseEvent(
  originalLength: number,
  sanitisedLength: number,
  now: () => number = Date.now,
): void;
```

Splitting build-from-emit is what lets a test assert on the payload without monkey-patching the clock or capturing `console.log`. The default parameter keeps every existing call site source-compatible.

```ts
// src/policy.ts
export function isAdmin(role: string): boolean;              // role === "admin"
export function isAdminOrOwner(role: string): boolean;       // was assertAdminB
export function isNotAdmin(role: string): boolean;           // was assertAdminC — name states the inversion
export function canEditBilling(role: string): boolean;
export function canSeeAuditLog(role: string): boolean;
export function canImpersonate(role: string): boolean;
```

`canEditBilling`, `canSeeAuditLog`, and `canImpersonate` currently have identical bodies. Keep all three as distinct exports — they're the same rule *today*, and collapsing them to one function destroys the seam where they'll differ tomorrow. Just make each one `return isAdmin(role)`.

### Files that would change

| file | change |
|---|---|
| `src/sanitize.ts` | **new** — `sanitize()` + `SanitizeOptions` |
| `src/telemetry.ts` | **new** — `recordSanitiseEvent()`, `buildSanitiseEvent()`, `SanitiseEvent` |
| `src/policy.ts` | **rewrite** — add `isAdmin` / `isAdminOrOwner` / `isNotAdmin`; the three `can*` bodies delegate |
| `src/sanitize-a.ts` | **delete** — body → `sanitize()`, `assertAdminA` → `isAdmin` |
| `src/sanitize-b.ts` | **delete** — `assertAdminB` → `isAdminOrOwner` (keeps the `"owner"` widening) |
| `src/sanitize-c.ts` | **delete** — `assertAdminC` → `isNotAdmin` (keeps the inversion) |
| `src/telemetry-a.ts` | **delete** |
| `src/telemetry-b.ts` | **delete** |

Straight deletion is safe here, no re-export shims needed: every finding reports `blast_radius_importers: 0`, and the package is `private` with no `main`/`exports` field — nothing inside or outside the fixture imports these modules. In a real repo with importers you'd keep the old files as one-line re-exports for a release before deleting.

Effort matches the report's `small` / `small` / `medium`. The medium is `crime_00003`, and now you know why: the sanitiser and telemetry work is mechanical, but the policy consolidation is a behaviour-preserving rename across three subtly different guards, and it's the only part of this refactor that can change what a user is allowed to do.

**Expected post-refactor scan:** all five findings clear. `crime_00001` and `crime_00002` go when the copies do; `crime_00004/5` go with the injected clock; `crime_00003` clears once `"admin"` appears in exactly one expression in one file.
